"use strict";(self.webpackChunkwebsite=self.webpackChunkwebsite||[]).push([["9263"],{139(e,n,t){t.r(n),t.d(n,{metadata:()=>o,default:()=>d,frontMatter:()=>s,contentTitle:()=>r,toc:()=>l,assets:()=>c});var o=JSON.parse('{"id":"reference/billing","title":"Billing","description":"connectivity \xb7 imported from live Hugo on 2026-10-07. Guides stay latest. Original page.","source":"@site/docs/reference/billing.md","sourceDirName":"reference","slug":"/reference/billing","permalink":"/personal-av-docs/docs/reference/billing","draft":false,"unlisted":false,"editUrl":"https://github.com/andreyjv/personal-av/tree/docs/documentation-revamp/PRD_documentation-revamp/website/docs/reference/billing.md","tags":[],"version":"current","sidebarPosition":99,"frontMatter":{"title":"Billing","sidebar_label":"Billing","sidebar_position":99,"edition":"connectivity","source":"https://doc.airvantage.net/connectivity/reference/billing","format":"md"}}'),i=t(4848),a=t(8453);let s={title:"Billing",sidebar_label:"Billing",sidebar_position:99,edition:"connectivity",source:"https://doc.airvantage.net/connectivity/reference/billing",format:"md"},r,c={},l=[];function h(e){let n={a:"a",p:"p",strong:"strong",...(0,a.R)(),...e.components};return(0,i.jsxs)(i.Fragment,{children:[(0,i.jsxs)(n.p,{children:[":::note Edition\n",(0,i.jsx)(n.strong,{children:"connectivity"})," \xb7 imported from live Hugo on 2026-10-07. Guides stay latest. ",(0,i.jsx)(n.a,{href:"https://doc.airvantage.net/connectivity/reference/billing",children:"Original page"}),".\n:::"]}),"\n",(0,i.jsxs)(n.p,{children:["Billing\nAdministration\nSubaccounts\nConnectivity Management\nDashboard\nMonitor\nConfigure\nAPI, Connectors & Other Services\nWeb Services API\nCloud Connectors\nCloud Connectors Format\nUSSD Gateway\nMore\nProduct page\nSearching...\nMatching results\nBilling\nThis page describes how connectivity is billed, where you can view current and historical invoices, and reviews the components of a connectivity invoice.\nInitializing Table Of Contents...\nHow is Sierra Wireless Connectivity priced?\nYou will be billed for your connectivity on a monthly basis. The billing cycle starts the first day of the month and always covers the full calendar month.\nSierra Wireless connectivity billing is split in monthly recurring charges and usages.\nDepending on your contract, you may have pay-per-use pricing or bundle pricing. The pay-per-use or bundle pricing is not only related to the monthly charges.\nThe usages are aggregated per pricing zone before rating (I.e., transforming usages into charged amounts). Each pricing zone covers a geographical area for which the usage prices are equivalent.\nPay-per-use pricing\nPay-per-use pricing is the simplest model. In addition to paying for actual usage, you pay a Monthly Recurring Charge (MRC) based on the number of active and suspended SIMs in the account at the beginning of the month. For activations in the middle of the month, no additional recurring charge is due, and the usages will be counted against the existing offer pool. Please refer to\nHow are initial activations billed\nfor more information.\nBundle pricing\nBundle pricing pools usage for all active subscriptions (and suspended SIMs for\nnon-seasonal offers\n) associated with the bundle. The contract defines the allotted data amount for each subscription. If a subscription consumes less than its allotted amount of data, the data can be consumed by other subscriptions in the bundle without overage charges. You will only pay an overage when the data consumption of the entire bundle exceeds the pool size computed at the beginning of the month. Overages beyond the pool size, as well as traffic in zones other than the one covered by the bundle, will be charged as pay-per-use.\nEach SIM can be associated with one single bundle defined on one specific pricing zone.\nThe pool size is computed based on the number of active subscriptions (and suspended subscriptions in case of non-seasonal offers) on the first day of the month. An active subscription contributes to the total data pool size by the volume of data usage defined in the contract. Usage is defined for the specified billing zone only. If you activate new subscriptions during the month, they will be added to the pool size on the first of the next month.\nExample: Your bundle includes 2MB per subscription and you have 27 activated subscriptions. These 27 subscriptions will share 54MB of data. Once the pool exceeds 54MB in a month, overage charges apply.\nPlease see\nFirst Activations Q&A\nor\nChange of Offer Q&A\nbelow for more specific details and examples.\nAdditional Fees\nIn addition to pay-per-use or bundle pricing, users may incur additional fees:\nSetup fees or one-time fees:\nAn invoice may include a one-time charge for a service or product started during the last invoice period. Example: Configuration of a dedicated APN, or an activation fee.\nPeriodic fees:\nRecurring monthly charges for a service or a product are charged a month in advance. Example: MRC for the pay-per-use model or bundle MRC for the bundle model\nUsage fees:\nUsage fees include traffic charges generated from the last invoice date until the current invoice date. Traffic fees are charged in arrears. If you have a bundle subscription, traffic fees will be split between the bundle charged upfront and potential overages charged in arrears.\nRetrieving Invoices\nA few days after the billing cycle is over (i.e., on the second or third working day of the month), invoices will be emailed to your billing contact from the email address\n",(0,i.jsx)(n.a,{href:"mailto:billing@sierrawireless.com",children:"billing@sierrawireless.com"}),"\n. Invoices can also be accessed at any time from your AirVantage account portal in the\nAccount>Billings>Invoices\nsection.\nFrom the\nLatest Invoices\nwidget in the\nAccount>Billings>Invoices\nsection, you can view your latest three invoices and the amounts billed on each, and download them as PDF files. Should you need to download multiple invoices at once, use the dedicated widget to select a specific period between three months ago and one year ago to download the invoices from that period.\nFor each month, beside the invoices, you can retrieve the reports that list different items that have been taken into account for the invoices of the same month.\nYou can use the\nNotifications\nwidget to include additional recipients on the invoice emails sent after each billing cycle:\nInvoice details\nThe invoice contains two sections:\nThe first section summarizes the costs per offer and any generic fees.\nThe second section details the charges for each offer, such as the monthly subscription fees based on the number of SIMs and the potential overages for the previous month.\nBelow is a sample invoice, with annotations to help you understand each cost line.\nSIM and usage reports\nFor each month, beside the invoices, you can retrieve reports that list different items that have been taken into account for that month\u2019s invoicing:\nthe list of orders, along with the Order ID, reference and quantity. We are also providing the Custom ID or External ID (that you can use to track the order ID in your own system) to ease your order checking\na data usage report that lists all the data usages that have been consumed during the month per SIM along with the offer and the pricing zone but also the labels and the company which you can use to dispatch the costs to different entities or your own customers.\nEager to see examples of use of the data usage report? Please check the\nHow to use usage reports to dispatch the connectivity costs among customers or business units?\nBilling Q & A\nHow are initial activations billed?\nWhen you activate your first subscription of a bundle offer, the pool computed at the beginning of the month is empty. Thus, you won\u2019t pay a complete monthly charge for the activation month, but you will be billed a pay-per-use fee that reflects the monthly consumption. You won\u2019t be penalized for end-of-month activations.\nAt the beginning of the next month, the pool size is computed based on the number of active SIMs in the bundle. The SIMs activated during the previous month now automatically contribute to the pool.\nIf you activate additional SIMs within that month, their usage is considered in the data pool for the bundle. Usage above the calculated pool amount will incur overages.\nWhen will I receive an invoice for my SIM orders?\nSIMs are invoiced the month after the order ships.\nHow is test-ready mode billed?\nFor each SIM activated on a test-ready mode (TRM) offer, you are charged a TRM activation fee at the time the SIM switches from test-ready to active. The TRM activation fee is a one-time charge per SIM.\nWhat happens to pricing with a change of offer?\nWhen changing a SIM from offer A to offer B:\nAll usage while the SIM is part of offer A (i.e., before the change) will be billed based on offer A pricing.\nAll usage while the SIM is on offer B (i.e., after the change) will be billed based on offer B pricing.\nAt the beginning of the month after the change, the pool size (or monthly subscription for a pay-per-use offer) will be reevaluated based on the updated number of SIMs in each offer. In this example, bundle A contains one less SIM and bundle B contains one more.\nAt the time of the offer change, if there are no active SIMs on the new offer then no pool exists. This means that the SIM that was moved will be billed on a pay-per-use basis for that month.\nWhat is a seasonal offer?\nAn offer is defined as\nseasonal\nif the contract mentions a specific MRC for suspended SIMs.\nFor each SIM suspended at the beginning of the billing cycle for a seasonal offer:\nyou will be charged a specific suspended MRC instead of the regular MRC.\nsuspended SIMs will not contribute to the pool size; the pool size in a seasonal offer is computed based only on the number of active subscriptions on the first day of the month.\nthe end of the engagement period for the suspended subscriptions will be postponed accordingly. The engagement period is the number of months for which you have paid the regular MRC for the subscription.\nA SIM on a seasonal offer may not be suspended for more than 12 months in a row.\nHow-Tos\nThis section provides you with helpful procedures for common tasks.\nHow to use usage reports to dispatch the connectivity costs among customers or business units?"]})]})}function d(e={}){let{wrapper:n}={...(0,a.R)(),...e.components};return n?(0,i.jsx)(n,{...e,children:(0,i.jsx)(h,{...e})}):h(e)}},8453(e,n,t){t.d(n,{R:()=>s,x:()=>r});var o=t(6540);let i={},a=o.createContext(i);function s(e){let n=o.useContext(a);return o.useMemo(function(){return"function"==typeof e?e(n):{...n,...e}},[n,e])}function r(e){let n;return n=e.disableParentContext?"function"==typeof e.components?e.components(i):e.components||i:s(e.components),o.createElement(a.Provider,{value:n},e.children)}}}]);